detail.abstract:
Contemporary societies have significantly expanded legal frameworks, institutional policies, and public discourse aimed at promoting gender equality. Nevertheless, despite this progress, persistent inequalities continue to emerge across social, educational, professional, cultural, and digital environments. This paper argues that one of the underlying causes of this paradox lies in the progressive erosion of human responsibility within contemporary societies.&nbsp;
<div>
 The article proposes that gender equality cannot be sustained exclusively through external regulation, institutional compliance, or formal policy implementation. Equality risks becoming rhetorical when disconnected from ethical responsibility, relational awareness, and conscious participation in social transformation processes.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 This paper introduces the concept of responsible equality, understood as a model of gender equality grounded not only in rights and institutional guarantees, but also in individual and collective responsibility. In this perspective, public policies should not function merely as rigid normative frameworks within which individuals operate, but as instruments capable of stimulating responsibility, ethical awareness, and transformative social behaviour.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Particular attention is dedicated to the effects of accelerated societies, digital environments, and artificial intelligence on human relationships and social accountability. The paper also reflects on the potential role of female leadership in fostering more ethical, relational, and human-centred approaches to social transformation.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The study contributes to interdisciplinary debates in gender studies, sociology, ethics, leadership studies, and artificial intelligence by proposing responsibility as a foundational cultural dimension for rebuilding authentic and sustainable gender equality in contemporary societies.
</div>
